### What is isolate-and-broadcast?

Isolate-and-broadcast is the most surgical technique when the hot keys are few and known. You split the join into two branches. First you filter the large table to just the hot keys and join that slice with a broadcast of the matching small-side rows. Then you filter to the cold keys and run a normal shuffle join. Finally you union the two results.

```java
package com.bank.spark;

import org.apache.spark.sql.Dataset;
import org.apache.spark.sql.Row;
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession;
import static org.apache.spark.sql.functions.*;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class IsolateBroadcastSkew {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder()
            .appName("NBI-Isolate-Broadcast").getOrCreate();

        List<String> hotKeys = Arrays.asList("POOLING_001", "SUSPENSE_002");

        Dataset<Row> transactions = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/transactions");
        Dataset<Row> accounts = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/accounts");

        // Hot path: broadcast the small side for hot keys
        Dataset<Row> hotTxns = transactions.filter(col("account_id").isin(hotKeys.toArray()));
        Dataset<Row> hotAccounts = accounts.filter(col("account_id").isin(hotKeys.toArray()));
        Dataset<Row> hotResult = hotTxns.join(broadcast(hotAccounts), "account_id");

        // Cold path: normal shuffle join
        Dataset<Row> coldTxns = transactions.filter(not(col("account_id").isin(hotKeys.toArray())));
        Dataset<Row> coldResult = coldTxns.join(accounts, "account_id");

        // Combine
        hotResult.unionByName(coldResult)
            .write().parquet("/data/bank/enriched");
    }
}
```

```text
Before: straggler task on POOLING_001 = 45 minutes
After:  hot path broadcast = 30 seconds, cold path median = 30 seconds
```

### Why does increasing parallelism help and when does it not?

Raising `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` increases the number of hash buckets, so moderately-hot keys that collide into the same partition are spread across more partitions. This helps when many keys are somewhat hot but no single key is a true mega-key.

It does not help a single mega-key because one key always hashes to exactly one partition regardless of partition count. If `POOLING_001` has 50 million rows, raising partitions from 200 to 2000 still sends all 50 million to one partition.

```java
package com.bank.spark;

import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession;

public class IncreaseParallelismDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder()
            .appName("NBI-Parallelism")
            .config("spark.sql.shuffle.partitions", "2000")
            .getOrCreate();

        // With 2000 partitions, moderately-hot merchant keys
        // that collided at 200 partitions are now spread out.
        // But POOLING_001 still goes to exactly one partition.
        spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/merchant_transactions")
            .groupBy("merchant_id")
            .count()
            .write().parquet("/data/bank/merchant_counts");
    }
}
```

### What is pre-aggregation and how does it reduce skew?

Pre-aggregation shrinks the data before it crosses the shuffle boundary. If you are joining transactions to accounts only to count per account, do the count first so each partition sends one partial count per key instead of millions of raw rows.

```java
package com.bank.spark;

import org.apache.spark.sql.Dataset;
import org.apache.spark.sql.Row;
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession;
import static org.apache.spark.sql.functions.*;

public class PreAggregationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder()
            .appName("NBI-PreAgg").getOrCreate();

        Dataset<Row> transactions = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/transactions");

        // Pre-aggregate: reduce 50M rows for POOLING_001 to one row
        Dataset<Row> preAgg = transactions
            .groupBy("account_id")
            .agg(sum("amount").alias("total_amount"),
                 count("*").alias("txn_count"));

        // Now join with accounts: the hot key has only 1 row
        Dataset<Row> accounts = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/accounts");
        Dataset<Row> result = preAgg.join(accounts, "account_id");
        result.write().parquet("/data/bank/account_summary");
    }
}
```

```text
Before pre-aggregation:
  POOLING_001 sends 50,000,000 rows across the shuffle

After pre-aggregation:
  POOLING_001 sends 1 row (total_amount, txn_count) across the shuffle
```

### How does changing the join strategy sidestep skew?

Only shuffle-based joins expose skew, because they hash keys to partitions. A broadcast hash join avoids shuffling the large side entirely by broadcasting the small side to all executors, so no partition becomes a straggler.

```java
package com.bank.spark;

import org.apache.spark.sql.Dataset;
import org.apache.spark.sql.Row;
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession;
import static org.apache.spark.sql.functions.*;

public class BroadcastJoinDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder()
            .appName("NBI-Broadcast-Join")
            .config("spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold", "100m")
            .getOrCreate();

        Dataset<Row> transactions = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/transactions");
        Dataset<Row> branches = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/branches"); // small table

        // Broadcast the small branches table to avoid shuffle
        Dataset<Row> enriched = transactions.join(
            broadcast(branches), "branch_id");
        enriched.write().parquet("/data/bank/enriched_with_branch");
    }
}
```

### What does redesigning the data model look like?

Redesigning the data model is the deepest fix. If a single pooling or suspense account is structurally hot, you split it into several sub-accounts at the source so no single key dominates.

```java
package com.bank.spark;

import org.apache.spark.sql.Dataset;
import org.apache.spark.sql.Row;
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession;
import static org.apache.spark.sql.functions.*;

public class DataModelRedesignDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder()
            .appName("NBI-Model-Redesign").getOrCreate();

        // Instead of one POOLING_001 account, the source system
        // now writes to POOLING_001_00 through POOLING_001_09
        Dataset<Row> transactions = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/transactions");

        // The hot key is now 10 separate keys, each with 1/10 the data
        Dataset<Row> accounts = spark.read().parquet("/data/bank/accounts");
        Dataset<Row> result = transactions.join(accounts, "sub_account_id");
        result.write().parquet("/data/bank/result");
    }
}
```

```text
Before redesign:  POOLING_001 = 50,000,000 rows (one partition)
After redesign:   POOLING_001_00..09 = 5,000,000 rows each (10 partitions)
```

### How do you compare these techniques?

| Technique | Best For | Fixes Mega-Key | Code Change | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Isolate-and-broadcast | Few known hot keys | Yes | Moderate | Low |
| Increase parallelism | Many moderately-hot keys | No | Config only | Low |
| Pre-aggregation | Downstream needs aggregate | Yes | Moderate | Low |
| Broadcast join | Small side fits in memory | Yes | Minimal | Low |
| Data model redesign | Structural hot key | Yes | High | High |

### What is the practical decision order?

Start with the cheapest and most surgical technique that fits the symptom.

1. Check if a broadcast join is possible. If the small side fits in memory, it eliminates skew with no shuffle.
2. Try pre-aggregation. If you only need an aggregate downstream, shrink the data before it crosses the shuffle.
3. Try increasing parallelism. If many keys are moderately hot, more partitions spread collisions.
4. Use isolate-and-broadcast for a few known hot keys that do not respond to the above.
5. Reserve data model redesign for a hot key that keeps causing incidents across many jobs.

```text
Decision order:
  1. Broadcast join possible?      → Use it (cheapest)
  2. Only need aggregate?           → Pre-aggregate
  3. Many moderately-hot keys?      → Increase parallelism
  4. Few known mega-keys?           → Isolate-and-broadcast
  5. Structural recurring problem?  → Redesign data model
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer combines these techniques deliberately. At the National Bank of India, a single job might use a broadcast join for the branch dimension, pre-aggregation for the merchant summary, and isolate-and-broadcast for the one corporate pooling account.

- Model redesign is the most durable fix but the most expensive to implement, so you reach for it when the same hot key causes incidents across many jobs.
- Monitor for new hot keys that appear over time. A key that was cold last quarter may become hot after a business change.
- Always verify in the Spark UI that the straggler task duration drops to roughly the median after applying the fix.
- Combine techniques rather than relying on a single silver bullet.

```java
// Production monitoring: check for skew in task duration
// Spark UI -> Stages -> Task Duration
// Goal: max_task_duration / median_task_duration < 3x
```
